Polling is the only option here — WebSocket upgrades 501 at the edge, and SSE/long-polling would need a connection held past the 10s function kill, which frozen serverless instances with no sticky routing can't sustain anyway.

Since `rollup` only changes once a minute (`jobs/rollup.md`) and `/api/metrics` is already cheap and `no-store` (`api/metrics.js`), just poll it from the client and re-render only when `computed_at` changes:

```js
// public/dashboard.js
async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

let lastComputedAt = null;
window.addEventListener('load', load);
setInterval(load, 15000);
```

Drop the refresh button, or leave it as a manual force-check — either works since `load()` becomes idempotent.

One decision left: poll interval. 15-20s gets updates felt within a minute without meaningfully increasing load on the 30ms/4KB endpoint; polling isn't worth aligning to the :00 rollup boundary given clock drift between client and job.

